    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p70-q72-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $pq=-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m_{OP}=p$ and $m_{OQ}=q$. Since $OP\\perp OQ$, $pq=-1$.",
          "worked": "Neither point is the origin, so $p,q\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{OP}&=\\frac{p^2}{p}=p\\\\m_{OQ}&=\\frac{q^2}{q}=q\\\\m_{OP}m_{OQ}&=-1\\\\pq&=-1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p70-q72-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ if the length of the hypotenuse $PQ$ is exactly $\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ units.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p70-q72-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P(\\sqrt2,2),\\ Q\\left(-\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " or ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right),\\ Q(-\\sqrt2,2)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p70-q72-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "q=-\\frac{1}{p}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " in the squared distance formula; ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "p^2=2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " or ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "p^2=\\frac{1}{2}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "From (a), $q=-\\frac{1}{p}$. Put $s=p^2+q^2$. Since $pq=-1$, $s\\ge2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}PQ^2&=(p-q)^2+(p^2-q^2)^2\\\\PQ^2&=(p-q)^2\\bigl(1+(p+q)^2\\bigr)\\\\PQ^2&=(s+2)(s-1)\\\\\\frac{27}{4}&=s^2+s-2\\\\0&=(2s-5)(2s+7)\\\\s&=\\frac52\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}p^2+\\frac1{p^2}&=\\frac52\\\\2p^4-5p^2+2&=0\\\\(2p^2-1)(p^2-2)&=0\\\\p^2&=\\frac12\\text{ or }2.\\end{align*}$$\nWith $P$ on the right branch and $Q$ on the left branch, $p>0$ and $q<0$. Thus\n$$P(\\sqrt2,2),\\quad Q\\left(-\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right)$$\nor\n$$P\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right),\\quad Q(-\\sqrt2,2).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 138
      }
    ]
